1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,885
Think of a group task
you have helped organize,

2
00:00:02,890 --> 00:00:05,273
such as a family gathering
or a volunteer day.

3
00:00:06,610 --> 00:00:10,670
Confusion usually starts when people
assume someone else knows what to do.

4
00:00:11,750 --> 00:00:14,830
Today we plan a fictional
Saturday computer-help session.

5
00:00:15,770 --> 00:00:16,803
You wrote the handout.

6
00:00:17,090 --> 00:00:18,150
Sam will review it.

7
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:21,604
Pat organizes the session
and keeps the shared copy.

8
00:00:22,190 --> 00:00:26,410
Alex runs the session on the day, and
twenty volunteers need to hear about it.

9
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:29,250
You will send a clear request,

10
00:00:29,770 --> 00:00:31,390
improve one shared copy,

11
00:00:32,250 --> 00:00:35,750
take part in a meeting,
and check an offer before you pay.

12
00:00:37,030 --> 00:00:38,610
The goal is not more messages.

13
00:00:39,290 --> 00:00:41,990
It is the right person
taking the right next step.

14
00:00:46,680 --> 00:00:48,701
Start by choosing how to reach someone.

15
00:00:49,300 --> 00:00:52,564
A detailed request that can wait
until tomorrow suits an email,

16
00:00:52,860 --> 00:00:54,725
because the details stay in writing.

17
00:00:55,720 --> 00:00:59,520
An urgent problem in this room
needs a word in person, right now.

18
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:03,842
A shared document suits work
that several people change.

19
00:01:05,140 --> 00:01:07,523
Then check which account
you are sending from.

20
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:10,600
In a new email, the From line shows it.

21
00:01:11,240 --> 00:01:12,120
A personal account

22
00:01:12,120 --> 00:01:12,940
and a volunteer

23
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:16,660
group account reach different people
and carry different expectations.

24
00:01:18,160 --> 00:01:22,150
Choose a channel for each of the two
messages on your worksheet, and say why.

25
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:24,200
Pause the video now.

26
00:01:35,293 --> 00:01:37,413
A useful email answers three questions.

27
00:01:38,293 --> 00:01:41,453
What should I do,
with which file, and by when?

28
00:01:42,743 --> 00:01:43,853
A subject such

29
00:01:43,853 --> 00:01:44,793
as Please

30
00:01:44,793 --> 00:01:46,033
review the computer-help

31
00:01:46,033 --> 00:01:50,653
handout by Thursday tells Sam the
job before the email is even opened.

32
00:01:52,033 --> 00:01:52,783
In the body,

33
00:01:53,053 --> 00:01:58,553
greet Sam, name the part to check, say
when you need a reply, and close politely.

34
00:02:00,253 --> 00:02:04,253
Spell out abbreviations, and drop
guesses about anyone's age or skill.

35
00:02:05,333 --> 00:02:07,153
Then read it as Sam would.

36
00:02:08,233 --> 00:02:10,513
Could Sam act without
asking you a question?

37
00:02:11,953 --> 00:02:13,127
Write the request to Sam,

38
00:02:13,873 --> 00:02:18,053
then rewrite the unclear message on your
worksheet so anyone could follow it.

39
00:02:19,313 --> 00:02:20,253
Pause the video now.

40
00:02:31,359 --> 00:02:34,339
Before you send,
read every recipient line.

41
00:02:35,579 --> 00:02:38,599
Sam needs to act, so Sam goes in To.

42
00:02:38,599 --> 00:02:40,412
Pat should know, so Pat goes in Cc.

43
00:02:41,419 --> 00:02:44,631
The volunteers do not need a
review request, so take them off.

44
00:02:45,959 --> 00:02:48,550
The notice to the volunteers
is a different email.

45
00:02:48,859 --> 00:02:50,879
There, Alex goes in To, Pat

46
00:02:50,879 --> 00:02:51,629
in Cc,

47
00:02:52,099 --> 00:02:56,399
and the twenty volunteers in Bcc, so
they cannot see one another's addresses.

48
00:02:57,699 --> 00:03:00,839
Bcc does not stop anyone
from forwarding it.

49
00:03:02,119 --> 00:03:07,119
If Pat chooses Reply all, it goes to
you and Alex, never to the Bcc names.

50
00:03:08,079 --> 00:03:12,379
Use Schedule send so an early
draft arrives at a sensible hour.

51
00:03:14,059 --> 00:03:16,494
Address the volunteer
notice on your worksheet.

52
00:03:16,499 --> 00:03:17,639
Pause the video now.

53
00:03:31,772 --> 00:03:36,532
Imagine three people editing three copies
of the handout and calling each one final.

54
00:03:37,512 --> 00:03:39,712
Nobody knows which instructions to follow.

55
00:03:41,512 --> 00:03:45,412
Instead, agree on one shared
copy and one place to keep it.

56
00:03:46,312 --> 00:03:48,229
Pat, the owner, makes the final call.

57
00:03:49,052 --> 00:03:49,802
You write.

58
00:03:49,872 --> 00:03:50,622
Sam reviews.

59
00:03:51,392 --> 00:03:53,827
Give each person only
the access the job needs.

60
00:03:54,852 --> 00:03:57,392
Sam can comment, and you and Pat can edit.

61
00:03:58,712 --> 00:04:01,352
Working at the same time
is called synchronous.

62
00:04:01,352 --> 00:04:04,252
Leaving comments for later
is called asynchronous.

63
00:04:04,252 --> 00:04:06,232
Neither is better.

64
00:04:06,232 --> 00:04:11,312
Pick the one that fits people's schedules,
and write the decision in the shared copy.

65
00:04:11,312 --> 00:04:17,752
With a partner, choose your roles,
your shared place, and how you will work.

66
00:04:17,752 --> 00:04:20,092
Pause the video now.

67
00:04:31,345 --> 00:04:32,685
Compare two comments.

68
00:04:33,705 --> 00:04:37,125
This is confusing gives the
writer nothing to act on.

69
00:04:37,985 --> 00:04:39,405
In step 2,

70
00:04:39,405 --> 00:04:45,125
add where the desk is, Room A on the first
floor, so first-time visitors can find it.

71
00:04:46,345 --> 00:04:49,605
That comment names a place,
a change, and a reason.

72
00:04:50,905 --> 00:04:56,965
In Word, select the step, choose Review,
then New Comment, and type it.

73
00:04:57,845 --> 00:05:01,785
The writer replies first,
then makes the change or explains why not.

74
00:05:02,665 --> 00:05:05,065
Resolve the comment only after that reply.

75
00:05:06,285 --> 00:05:08,685
A resolved comment is hidden, not deleted.

76
00:05:10,005 --> 00:05:12,985
Comment on the work, never on the person.

77
00:05:13,985 --> 00:05:14,805
Trade seats,

78
00:05:15,665 --> 00:05:20,765
add one useful comment, then reply to and
resolve the comment on your own handout.

79
00:05:21,685 --> 00:05:22,865
Pause the video now.

80
00:05:37,052 --> 00:05:38,917
Before a meeting, test your headset.

81
00:05:39,572 --> 00:05:40,511
In Windows Settings,

82
00:05:40,732 --> 00:05:44,992
under Sound, choose the headset as
the input and speak during the test.

83
00:05:46,532 --> 00:05:47,282
In the meeting,

84
00:05:47,412 --> 00:05:51,752
stay muted unless you are speaking, and
raise your hand when the host asks for it.

85
00:05:52,432 --> 00:05:53,852
Turn on captions if they help.

86
00:05:55,652 --> 00:05:56,732
A webinar is different.

87
00:05:57,252 --> 00:05:58,910
The host controls who can speak,

88
00:05:59,152 --> 00:06:02,052
so questions go in the
Q and A box or the chat.

89
00:06:03,192 --> 00:06:07,812
Ask before you record anyone, especially
a group sharing personal stories.

90
00:06:08,972 --> 00:06:10,837
If your sound fails partway through,

91
00:06:11,172 --> 00:06:15,212
use the backup the host gave you,
such as the chat or the shared notes.

92
00:06:17,077 --> 00:06:21,417
A community page can point you to
a pantry, a class, or an event.

93
00:06:22,757 --> 00:06:24,837
A member's post is a lead, not a fact.

94
00:06:25,637 --> 00:06:28,697
It might be old,
about a different place, or simply wrong.

95
00:06:29,917 --> 00:06:33,699
Read the group's rules, and keep
private details out of public questions.

96
00:06:34,577 --> 00:06:35,497
For hours,

97
00:06:35,977 --> 00:06:37,437
eligibility, or what to bring,

98
00:06:37,897 --> 00:06:41,617
check the organization's own
page or call its listed number.

99
00:06:42,497 --> 00:06:46,097
That is the same habit you
used with AI answers in Week 2.

100
00:06:46,937 --> 00:06:51,197
Open the Mission 4C practice page
and practice the meeting controls.

101
00:06:52,437 --> 00:06:56,837
Then list your meeting habits and one
community claim you would check yourself.

102
00:06:58,277 --> 00:06:59,337
Pause the video now.

103
00:07:13,427 --> 00:07:15,587
Here are two fictional streaming offers.

104
00:07:16,647 --> 00:07:20,827
Offer A is free for 30 days,
then 6 dollars a month until you cancel.

105
00:07:21,687 --> 00:07:24,887
Offer B is 60 dollars a year, paid now.

106
00:07:26,227 --> 00:07:28,387
Free does not mean free forever.

107
00:07:29,327 --> 00:07:30,360
After the first month,

108
00:07:30,687 --> 00:07:36,047
A charges you every month,
so a full year of A costs 66 dollars.

109
00:07:37,227 --> 00:07:38,025
Before you agree,

110
00:07:38,407 --> 00:07:39,187
find the seller,

111
00:07:39,667 --> 00:07:43,407
the price now, the price later,
the renewal date, and how to cancel.

112
00:07:45,327 --> 00:07:47,147
Deleting the app does not cancel

113
00:07:47,147 --> 00:07:51,747
the subscription. Pay with a credit card,
which lets you dispute a wrong charge.

114
00:07:51,747 --> 00:07:55,587
Person-to-person apps
are for people you know.

115
00:07:56,587 --> 00:08:01,947
Work out both offers for 3 months and
for 12, and write two questions to ask.

116
00:08:03,127 --> 00:08:04,066
Pause the video now.

117
00:08:18,089 --> 00:08:21,249
Good coordination leaves
a trail anyone can follow.

118
00:08:22,569 --> 00:08:23,589
By the end of today,

119
00:08:23,989 --> 00:08:27,369
Sam knows what to review and by when.
Pat can see the notice

120
00:08:27,369 --> 00:08:29,545
without the volunteers seeing one another.

121
00:08:29,689 --> 00:08:30,829
Everyone works from one

122
00:08:30,829 --> 00:08:34,649
shared copy, and every comment
ends with a reply and a decision.

123
00:08:36,009 --> 00:08:40,309
In meetings, you test first,
take turns, and ask before recording.

124
00:08:41,309 --> 00:08:45,729
Before you pay for anything, you know what
it will cost in a year and how to stop it.

125
00:08:46,389 --> 00:08:48,469
Pick one habit to use this week:

126
00:08:48,949 --> 00:08:50,329
a clearer subject line,

127
00:08:50,829 --> 00:08:55,349
fewer recipients, one shared copy,
or a check before you pay.
